20:	/**
21:	 * <p>Like {@link #handleOnServer}, but only for whoever is DM: resolves the sender, checks the
22:	 * operator permission, and passes the already-validated player. If they aren't one, the packet is
23:	 * discarded without doing anything.</p>
24:	 *
25:	 * <p>Exists because the guard was copied <b>22 times</b>, word for word, across messages that only a
26:	 * DM should be able to send. And that isn't ugliness: it's a permission check that has to be
27:	 * remembered. A new message that forgets it doesn't fail or warn — it lets any player delete dungeon
28:	 * pieces, spawn monsters, or edit content, because the client can send the packet regardless of
29:	 * whether the menu is even open. Here it can't be forgotten: either you request the player through
30:	 * this door, or you don't have one.</p>
31:	 */
32:	public static void handleOnServerAsDm(NetworkEvent.Context context, java.util.function.Consumer<net.minecraft.server.level.ServerPlayer> action) {
33:		handleOnServer(context, () -> {
34:			net.minecraft.server.level.ServerPlayer dm = context.getSender();
35:			if (dm == null) return;
36:			//A notice is sent instead of discarding silently: the DM Panel is deliberately opened for
